\section{Overview}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Objectives and overview}
  \mode<article>{
  This section introduces split-plot designs, which are heavily used when logistical or cost constraints prevent us from fully crossing and randomizing our factors in a standard way.
  Building on our knowledge of nesting and random effects, split-plot designs introduce the idea of having more than one size of experimental unit and more than one randomization in the same experiment.
}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item This material is based on Chapter~16 of the textbook ``A First Course in Design and Analysis of Experiments" \citep{oehlert2000}.
    \item Chapter outcomes include:
      \begin{itemize}
        \item Students will be able to identify split-plot designs and explain how they differ from CRD, RCB, and Nested designs.
        \item Students will be able to distinguish between whole-plot factors and split-plot factors.
        \item Students will understand how to formulate mixed-effects models that account for multiple randomizations.
        \item Students will be able to analyze split-plot data using \texttt{lme4} in R.
      \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{What Is a Split Plot?}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{The Need for Split Plots}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item We generally choose a split-plot design when some factors are more difficult or expensive to vary than others.
    \item \textbf{Key Feature 1:} There are \alert{two sizes of experimental units}.
    \item \textbf{Key Feature 2:} There are \alert{two separate randomizations}.
  \end{itemize}
  \begin{block}{Definition: Whole Plots and Split Plots}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \textbf{Whole plots:} The larger experimental units used for the first randomization.
      \item \textbf{Split plots:} The smaller units (subdivisions of the whole plots) used for the second randomization.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  \framebreak
  \begin{exampleblock}{Example: Corn Varieties and Irrigation}
    We want to determine the effects of 4 corn varieties and 3 levels of irrigation on yield.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Sprinklers irrigate large areas. It is logistically difficult to use small units for irrigation.
      \item \textbf{Whole plots:} Divide land into 6 large plots. Randomly assign irrigation levels (2 plots per level). Irrigation is the \alert{whole-plot factor}.
      \item \textbf{Split plots:} Divide each whole plot into 4 smaller split plots. Randomly assign the 4 corn varieties within each whole plot. Variety is the \alert{split-plot factor}.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{exampleblock}
  
  \framebreak
  
  \begin{itemize}
    \item A split plot is essentially a restricted randomization.
    \item All split plots within a given whole plot must receive the \emph{same level} of the whole-plot factor.
    
    \begin{block}{General Rule of Thumb: More precision for smaller units}
    Because the \alert{whole-plot} units act like \emph{blocks} for the split-plots, comparisons involving the split-plot factor are generally \alert{more precise} than comparisons involving the whole-plot factor.
    \end{block}
    
  \end{itemize}
  \mode<article>{
  You cannot distinguish a split-plot design from a completely randomized factorial design just by looking at a table of factor levels and responses.
  You \emph{must} know how the randomization was done! Always follow the randomization to identify the units and type of experimental design.
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Example: Piano Ratings}
  \begin{block}{Testing Pianos}
    A music student wants to evaluate pianos: a baby grand and a concert grand from each of 4 manufacturers.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item 40 students divided into 8 panels of 5 students each.
      \item 2 panels randomly assigned to each manufacturer.
      \item Each panel goes to a concert hall for a session. They listen to the baby grand and concert grand of their assigned manufacturer in a random order.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}

  \framebreak

  \textbf{Identify the Split-Plot Structure:}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item What are the whole plots? \answer{\textbf{The Panels.}}
    \item What is the whole-plot factor? \answer{\textbf{Manufacturer.}}
    \item What are the split plots? \answer{\textbf{The individual piano performances (or listening slots within a session).}}
    \item What is the split-plot factor? \answer{\textbf{Piano Type (Baby vs. Concert).}}
  \end{itemize}
  
  \mode<article>{
  Notice that the individual panelists are \emph{measurement units}, not experimental units. 
  The response for any session must be some summary (like an average) of the 5 panelist ratings, or they must be modeled with an additional nested error term.
}

\end{frame}

\section{Analysis of a Split Plot}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Modeling the Split Plot}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Golden Rule:} We assume that there is a random effect for \alert{every randomization}.
    \item For a basic split-plot, we have:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item A random effect for each whole plot (Whole-Plot Error).
      \item A random effect for each split plot (Split-Plot Error).
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{itemize}
  \framebreak
  Let factor $A$ be the whole-plot factor, and factor $B$ be the split-plot factor.
$$
y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \eta_{k(i)} + \beta_j + (\alpha\beta)_{ij} + \epsilon_{k(ij)}
$$

  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha_i$: Fixed effect of whole-plot factor $A$.
    \item $\eta_{k(i)}$: Random error for whole plot $k$ (nested in $A$). \textbf{(Whole-Plot Error)}
    \item $\beta_j$: Fixed effect of split-plot factor $B$.
    \item $(\alpha\beta)_{ij}$: Interaction between whole-plot and split-plot factors.
    \item $\epsilon_{k(ij)}$: Random error for the split plot. 
    \textbf{(Split-Plot Error)}
  \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak
  
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Why is the whole-plot error nested in treatment? \answer{In a split-plot design, the treatments ($A$ and $B$) are crossed, but the experimental units (the whole plots and split plots themselves) are nested within treatment.
    Each whole plot only receives \textbf{one} level of Factor $A$; consequently, whole-plot 1 assigned to $A = 1$ is a completely different physical unit than whole-plot 1 assigned to $A = 2$.
    We can encode the data with unique IDs for every whole plot, or reuse labels $1, \ldots, r$ within each level of $A$---how it is encoded changes the ``correct'' syntax in R.}
    
    \mode<presentation>{\vspace{4cm}} 
    
    \item Why is there an interaction effect? \answer{Because the treatment factors $A$ and $B$ are \textbf{crossed}: every level of the whole-plot factor ($A$) is paired with every level of the split-plot factor ($B$).
    Unlike a standard nested design, the nesting in a split-plot is in the \emph{random error terms} (the physical units), \emph{not} the treatment effects.
    Specifically, the whole-plot error is nested in treatment $A$, and the split-plot error is nested in the $A \times B$ combination.}
  \end{itemize}
  
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Decomposing the Variation}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item A useful heuristic is to split the total variation into two bundles, and sometimes writing the equation on two lines:
    \begin{align*}
      y_{ijk} =& \mu + \alpha_i + \eta_{k(i)} \\ 
      & + \beta_j + (\alpha\beta)_{ij} + \epsilon_{k(ij)}
    \end{align*}
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item \textbf{Between Whole Plots:} Variation due to effects that impact complete whole plots (Whole-plot treatments, whole-plot blocks, whole-plot error).
      \item \textbf{Within Whole Plots (Between Split Plots):} Variation due to effects acting on individual split plots (Split-plot treatments, interaction, split-plot error).
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{itemize}
  \mode<article>{
Notice why the interaction between the whole- and split-plot factors sits at the split-plot level: The interaction describes how the split-plot treatment effects change across levels of the whole-plot factor.
Because this involves changing levels of the split-plot factor, it must be evaluated at the lower (split-plot) level.
}
\end{frame}

\section{Split Plots in R}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks,fragile]{Example: Anxiety, Tension, and Memory}
  
  We wish to study the effects of anxiety and muscular tension on four different types of memory. The low-anxiety group is told that they will be awarded \$10 if they remember accurately, and the high-anxiety group is told they will be awarded \$100 if they remember accurately.
  Participants must hold a spring (low or high tension) to keep a buzzer from going off during the trial. 
  
  \begin{block}{Memory Experiment}
    12 subjects are randomly assigned to one of 4 Anxiety-Tension combinations (Low/High Anxiety $\times$ Low/High Tension).
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Each subject does 4 memory trials testing different types of memory.
      \item Trial type (1-4) is randomized to the 4 trials for a given subject.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  
  \framebreak
  
  \mode<article>{
  This is a split-plot design, with factorial treatment structure for the whole-plot.
  The first level of randomization is the assign the anxiety-tension combinations to each subject. 
  Even though we will have four responses from each subject, the randomization is restricted so that all four of those responses will be at the same anxiety-tension combination.
  Thus, \textbf{Anxiety} and \textbf{Tension} are the whole-plot treatment factors, the whole-plot units being the subjects.
  
  Each subject will do four memory trials. The trial type is randomized to the four trials for a given subject. Thus, the four trials for a subject are the split-plot units, and the trial type is the split-plot treatment. 
  }
  
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Whole plots:} \answer{Subjects.}
    \item \textbf{Whole-plot factors:} \answer{Anxiety and Tension.}
    \item \textbf{Split plots:} \answer{Trials within subjects.}
    \item \textbf{Split-plot factor:} \answer{Memory Type.}
  \end{itemize}

\end{frame}

\section{Analysis example in R: Anxiety}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks,fragile]{Analysis in R}

First we would like to load and prepare the data:

<<memory-example, eval=TRUE, message=FALSE>>=
memory <- read.csv('memory.csv') 

memory$anxiety <- factor(memory$anxiety)
memory$tension <- factor(memory$tension)
memory$mem.type <- factor(memory$mem.type)
memory$subject <- factor(memory$subject)
@ 

\framebreak

Next, it is important to make sure we know how the data are encoded.
Check however you want, but we notice for this data: 
\begin{itemize}
  \item Subject has been encoded only $1, 2, 3$, whereas the actual experiment has 12 subjects.
  \item The subjects can be identified by their treatment combination: there are two whole-plot level treatments: Anxiety and Tension, each with two levels, for a total of 4 combinations.
  \item 3 subjects are randomly assigned to each of these 4 combinations, so \emph{subject is nested in treatment}.
\end{itemize}

\framebreak

<<memory-ANOVA, eval=TRUE, message=FALSE>>=
library(lme4)
library(lmerTest)

mod_memory <- lmer(
  num.errors ~ anxiety * tension * mem.type + 
    (1 | subject:anxiety:tension),
  data = memory
)
@

  \framebreak

  \begin{itemize}
    \item By using \texttt{(1 | subject:anxiety:tension)}, we correctly identify subject and avoid pseudo-replication, while explicitly adding the \alert{Whole-Plot Error} term $\eta_{k(i)}$.
    \item The residual error automatically handles the \alert{Split-Plot Error} term $\epsilon_{k(ij)}$.
    \item \textbf{Important:} Remember from the nested module---If \texttt{subject} was given a unique ID (Explicit IDs: 1 to 12), we could just write \texttt{(1 | subject)}.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak
  
<<print-Anova-Table, size='scriptsize',custom.width=75>>=
anova(mod_memory)
@
  
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks,fragile]{R analysis: Continued}
  We investigate these results further, with the following findings:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item At the split-plot level, the effect of type is highly significant.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item No interaction with type (anxiety by type, tension by type, or the three-way interaction) appears significant.
      We could do a post-hoc inference on this if we would like.
    \end{itemize}
    \item At the whole-plot level, we did not find significant main-effects for anxiety or tension. \emph{However}, there is a moderately significant interaction effect. Let's investigate this interaction further.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak
  
<<emmean-interaction, message=FALSE>>=
library(emmeans)
em_mod <- emmeans(mod_memory, ~ anxiety*tension)
emmip(em_mod, anxiety ~ tension)
@


\mode<article>{In the plot above, we see that the most errors are made when both factors are at level 2; the difference in means is small, and we didn't consider and multiple testing, so the marginally significant p-value may just be due to multiple testing.

One last note is regarding model assumptions. The non-constant variance assumption looks to be slightly problematic, though no transformation really fixes it, so we still just interpret on the original scale, keeping in mind the potential for inflated Type-I errors.}
  
\end{frame}

\section{Extensions}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Fancier Split Plots}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item Just like factorial designs, split plots can be extended to fit logistical needs:
    \item \textbf{Blocking at the whole-plot level:} Often, whole plots are grouped into blocks (e.g., RCB) before treatments are assigned.
    \item \textbf{Split-Split Plots:} We can split the units again!
    
    \mode<article>{\textbf{Idea:} We may have 3 factors, $A, B, C$. We start with a standard split-plot, that is we have $n$ whole-plots to which we assign the levels of $A$. We split the whole-plots into distinct units, which we assign to the levels of $B$. So far, this is exactly the same as a standard split-plot. We can then split the split-plot units further to assign the levels of $C$! We call these smallest level of experimental units split-split-plots. This requires \textbf{three} sizes of experimental units and \textbf{three} randomizations!
}
    
  \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak
 
 \begin{itemize}
 
  \item An experiment studies the effect of nitrogen and weeds on plant growth in wetlands. There are 3 factors: Nitrogen level (4-levels), Weeds (no weeds, species 1, species 2), and two physical treatments (clipping vs no-clipping).
  
  \item We have 8 trays, each tray holds 3 artificial wetlands. Four of the trays are placed on a table near the door of the greenhouse, and the other four trays are placed on a table in the center. On each table, we randomly assign one of the trays to each of the four nitrogen treatments. Within each tray, we randomly assign the wetlands to the three weed treatments.
  
  \item Each wetland is then split in half, with clipping type randomly assigned to one half.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak
  
  
  \begin{block}{Split-Split Plot Summary}
  
    This is a \textbf{blocked split-split-plot} design.
  
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \textbf{Blocking variable (Table)}: The blocking occurred for the highest EUs. We block by table location, and each table gets exactly one of each nitrogen treatment (RCBD). 
      \item \textbf{Whole plot (Tray):} Assigned a Nitrogen level. \mode<article>{Everything in a given tray has the same level of nitrogen, so the trays are whole plots, and the nitrogen is the whole-plot factor.}
      \item \textbf{Split plot (Wetland):} Assigned a Weed treatment. \mode<article>{Each of the measurements for a given wetland have the same weed treatment, so the wetlands are split-plots, and weed assignment is the split-plot factor.}
      \item \textbf{Split-Split plot (Half-Wetland):} Assigned a Clipping treatment. \mode<article>{Each wetland half gets its own clipping treatment, so they are the split-split plot units, with clipping being the factor.}
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Strip Plots and Repeated Measures}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Strip Plots (Split Block):} Occurs when the units \alert{cross} rather than nest. 
    E.g., You randomize Tool Brand to Lathes (Rows), and Material Supplier to Days (Columns). 
    Both are restricted randomizations, but they cross each other.
    \item \textbf{Repeated Measures:} Looks like a split-plot (Subjects = Whole plots, Time = Split plots), but \alert{lacks a randomization} at the split-plot level because time cannot be randomized. 
    Analyzing it like a split-plot requires specific assumptions about the correlation structure (e.g., compound symmetry).
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

